      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine $\\tan V$ in triangle \\( VWX \\) where \\( VX = \\sqrt{13} \\) and \\( VW = 3 \\), we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the length of \\( WX \\)**:\n   Given that \\( VWX \\) is a right triangle with \\( \\angle VWX = 90^\\circ \\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\( WX \\). The Pythagorean theorem states:\n   \\[\n   VX^2 = VW^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt{13})^2 = 3^2 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   13 = 9 + WX^2\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( WX^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 13 - 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides yields:\n   \\[\n   WX = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\tan V\\)**:\n   In a right triangle, the tangent of an angle is given by the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side. For \\(\\angle V\\) in triangle \\( VWX \\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{WX}{VW}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute the known values**:\n   Substitute \\( WX = 2 \\) and \\( VW = 3 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
